<h1 class="article-title">New SyFud Plant in Liepāja Will Significantly Increase Latvia’s Fish Processing Export Capacity</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>The opening of the new SyFud plant in Liepāja will substantially increase and diversify Latvia’s fish processing production and export capacity. The fish-processing factory opened on 31 October, following total investments of approximately EUR 80 million in infrastructure and technologies. Over time, its annual production value could reach EUR 250 million.</p><p>SIA Syfud, operating under the Port Lite brand, acquired the site of the former Liepāja fish-canning factory in 2020 and began a major investment project implemented in several phases. Three production buildings have now been constructed and equipped with new, modern machinery ensuring a safe, environmentally responsible and efficient fish-processing operation.</p><p>“Latvia, and Liepāja in particular, has longstanding and robust fish-processing traditions. We are proud to work in both this sector and this city, but it also brings great responsibility. Our aim in planning the Liepāja factory was to produce new, modern fish products demanded in European and other Western markets. We will not compete with traditional Latvian fish processors; instead, we specialise in new products, contributing to Liepāja, the industry and Latvia’s economy as a whole,” says company owner Sigits Ambrazevičs.</p><p>Minister of Agriculture Armands Krauze says: “The new fish-processing plant in Liepāja is the result of successful implementation of Latvia’s fisheries development policy, which makes it possible to channel substantial, targeted EU support towards investment and development in Latvia. It is equally important that EU-supported investment reaches the regions, helping the industry grow more evenly across the country. Liepāja has always been one of Latvia’s fish-processing centres, and I am pleased that, thanks to entrepreneurs and EU funding, fish processing and fishing continue to thrive here. EU support and projects like this provide a good example for both established and aspiring entrepreneurs who wish to modernise or start businesses that can contribute not only to fisheries but to the entire economy and employment.”</p><p>Syfud produces two main categories of goods: crab sticks and other surimi products, and salmon products. At full capacity, the factory will be able to produce 1,000 tonnes of surimi and 1,000 tonnes of salmon products each month, reaching turnover of up to EUR 250 million annually. Output will be increased gradually: crab-stick production began in 2021, and the installed lines are expected to reach full capacity within three years. The overwhelming majority of Syfud products are intended for export, with approximately 10% potentially remaining in the Baltic market. The main export destinations are Germany, France, Italy, Spain and other EU countries; discussions are also under way with partners in the United States.</p><p>To maintain consistently high quality and meet customer demand, the factory uses state-of-the-art equipment from Marel, Reich and Multivac, employs experienced, highly qualified specialists and will source production raw materials from Norway and the United States.</p><p>“The efficient balance between product quality and cost enables Syfud to rank among Europe’s top three smoked-salmon producers. This means a new, large and highly competitive player is entering the processed-salmon market, bringing additional capacity and value to Latvia’s fish-processing industry,” says S. Ambrazevičs.</p><p>The Syfud factory currently employs 150 people. The workforce is expected to grow to 200 by the end of the year and to around 800 by the end of 2027, when the plant is expected to reach full production capacity.</p><p>“The new Syfud fish-processing plant is an ambitious project that revives Liepāja’s fish-processing traditions and creates hundreds of new jobs. Through large-scale investment and cutting-edge production technologies, it strengthens our city as a regional centre for the economy and innovation and improves Latvia’s competitiveness on world markets,” says Liepāja Mayor Gunārs Ansiņš.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ Manager Uldis Hmieļevskis says: “Syfud is the 25th factory built in the Liepāja SEZ over the past seven years and one of two operating in food production. Each new factory demonstrates the quality of Liepāja’s business environment and creates jobs that make a significant contribution to the city budget, helping establish Liepāja as an important centre not only for the regional economy but also for education, culture and sport.”</p><p>The factory’s salmon range includes smoked, salted and frozen salmon and trout products supplied in various packages, including vacuum packs and modified-atmosphere packaging (MAP). Salmon is sourced from organic and wild fisheries as well. Its surimi range includes sticks, pieces, shrimp-style products, and other fresh and frozen products in vacuum or alternative packaging. “We will have almost everything you can imagine that retailers and food-service businesses might need,” adds S. Ambrazevičs.</p><p>More than EUR 75 million excluding VAT has been invested in building and equipping the new factory. The project was co-financed through the European Maritime, Fisheries and Aquaculture Fund measure “Processing of Fishery and Aquaculture Products”, administered by the Rural Support Service, as well as financing from Rietumu Banka.</p><p>“To build a factory on this scale, investments — including EU funding in this case — are only one prerequisite. Behind it all are people with an idea, an understanding of how a modern company should operate and the determination to compete in European and global markets. Fisheries is a sector whose products can proudly carry Latvia’s name around the world. I wish the company continued growth,” says Indulis Āboliņš, Acting Director of the Rural Support Service.</p><p>Jeļena Buraja, Chair of the Management Board of Rietumu Banka, emphasises: “Financing local businesses is a cornerstone of our bank’s strategy, and manufacturing has a special place in it. I am convinced manufacturers must receive support, because they create added value and jobs, expand export potential and generate tax revenue. Such companies are crucial to the development of the economy and the whole country; they foster growth in Latvia’s regions and improve people’s welfare. I wish the new factory success and hope that very soon the people of Liepāja, and all of us in Latvia, will be proud of its achievements.”</p><p>SIA Syfud is a Latvian-registered company with Lithuanian capital. Its sole owner and ultimate beneficial owner is Sigits Ambrazevičs, a well-known Lithuanian entrepreneur with more than 25 years of experience in seafood processing and trade. He owns several businesses and is also an active patron of the arts and sport.</p></div>
